.TH LSSEQ 1 "2026" "lsseq 5.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lsseq \- list directory contents, condensing image, cache, and movie sequences into single entries
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lsseq
[\fB\-h\fR | \fB\-\-help\fR]
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
[\fIFILE\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lsseq
lists directory contents much like
.BR ls (1),
except that it condenses image, cache, and movie sequences into a
single compact entry each, rather than listing every frame
individually.
.PP
Filenames that are part of a sequence are assumed to be of the form:
.PP
.RS
.I descriptiveName\fR.\fIframeNum\fR.\fIimgExtension
.RE
.PP
where
.I imgExtension
is drawn from a built-in list of recognized image, cache, and movie
file extensions (see
.B \-\-img\-ext
below), or from a colon-separated list supplied via the
.B LSSEQ_IMAGE_EXTENSION ,
.B LSSEQ_MOV_EXTENSION ,
or
.B LSSEQ_CACHE_EXTENSION
environment variables.
.B lsseq
also accepts an underscore in place of the dot separating
.I descriptiveName
from
.I frameNum
when
.B \-\-loose\-num\-separator
is given.
.PP
.B lsseq
first lists all non-sequence files exactly as
.BR ls (1)
would (minus anything that is part of a sequence), then lists all
detected sequences in condensed form:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ lsseq
[output of /bin/ls minus image/cache/movie sequences]
[list of condensed sequences]
.fi
.RE
.PP
For example, given a directory containing
.IR aaa.097.tif " through " aaa.103.tif
with frame 099 missing, plus two unrelated files:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ls
aaa.097.tif  aaa.100.tif  aaa.102.tif  bar.pdf
aaa.098.tif  aaa.101.tif  aaa.103.tif  foo.txt
$ lsseq
bar.pdf  foo.txt
aaa.[097\-103].tif m:[99]
.fi
.RE
.PP
The
.B m:[99]
annotation reports that frame 99 is missing from the sequence. See
.B DISPLAY OF ERROR FRAMES
below for the other annotations
.B lsseq
can report (zero-length frames, undersized/bad frames, and badly
padded frame numbers).
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Miscellaneous options
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Show the help message and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the program's version number and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-silent ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress error and warning messages.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of options; all subsequent arguments are treated as positional
file names, even if they begin with a dash.
.SS Sequence interpretation
.TP
.B \-\-split\-sequence
Print sequences with missing frames as if they were multiple,
separate sequences sharing the same name but with different frame
ranges. This affects only how a sequence is printed, not how
sequence times are calculated; sorting by time may not behave as
expected when combined with this option.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-split\-sequence
Consider all frames sharing the same name as one sequence.
(Default.)
.TP
.BR \-\-strict\-num\-separator ", " \-s
Strictly require
.RB \(aq . \(aq
(dot) as the separator between
.I descriptiveName
and
.IR frameNum .
(Default.)
.TP
.BR \-\-loose\-num\-separator ", " \-l
Additionally allow
.RB \(aq _ \(aq
(underscore) as the separator between
.I descriptiveName
and
.IR frameNum .
.SS Display of error frames
.TP
.BR \-\-show\-missing ", " \-m
Show the list of missing frames as
.BR m:[ <list> ] .
(Default.)
.TP
.BR \-\-skip\-missing ", " \-M
Do not show the list of missing frames.
.TP
.BR \-\-show\-zero ", " \-z
Show the list of zero-length frames as
.BR z:[ <list> ] .
(Default.)
.TP
.BR \-\-skip\-zero ", " \-Z
Do not show the list of zero-length frames.
.TP
.BR \-\-show\-bad\-frames ", " \-b
Show frames smaller than the minimum good-frame size (see
.BR \-\-good\-frame\-min\-size )
as
.BR b:[ <list> ] .
.TP
.BR \-\-skip\-bad\-frames ", " \-B
Do not show the list of potentially bad (undersized) frames.
(Default.)
.TP
.BI \-\-good\-frame\-min\-size " BYTES"
Any frame smaller than
.I BYTES
is considered a bad frame. Accepts short forms such as
.B 1K
(1024) or
.BR 1.5K .
Default: 512.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-bad\-padding
Report frame numbers whose padding is inconsistent with the rest of
the sequence, as
.BR p:[ <list> ] .
(Default.)
.TP
.B \-\-skip\-bad\-padding
Do not show the list of badly padded frames.
.TP
.B \-\-combine\-lists
Combine the missing, zero, bad, and bad-padding lists into a single
list, reported as
.BR e:[ <list> ] .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-combine\-lists
Do not combine the error lists. (Default.)
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-error\-lists ", " \-n
Suppress all of the error-frame lists above in one go. If a more
specific option such as
.B \-\-show\-bad\-padding
appears
.I after
this one on the command line, that list is shown after all; option
processing is left to right, as with all argparse-style tools.
.SS Sequence-category filters
.TP
.BR \-\-img\-ext ", " \-i
Print the built-in lists of recognized image, movie, and cache file
extensions (and the corresponding environment variables to override
them), then exit.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-all\-files
List all sequences plus regular
.BR ls (1)
output. (Default.)
.TP
.BR \-\-only\-sequences ", " \-o
Omit regular
.BR ls (1)
output; list only sequences.
.TP
.BR \-\-only\-images ", " \-O
List strictly image sequences (no movies or caches).
.TP
.B \-\-not\-images
Do not treat image files as sequences; they are listed
individually as regular files instead. If
.B \-\-only\-sequences
is also given, image files are omitted from the listing
altogether, since that option suppresses the regular-file output
that
.B \-\-not\-images
would otherwise fall back to.
.TP
.B \-\-only\-movies
List strictly movie sequences (no images or caches).
.TP
.B \-\-not\-movies
Do not treat movie files as sequences; they are listed
individually as regular files instead. If
.B \-\-only\-sequences
is also given, movie files are omitted from the listing
altogether, since that option suppresses the regular-file output
that
.B \-\-not\-movies
would otherwise fall back to.
.TP
.B \-\-only\-caches
List strictly cache sequences (no images or movies).
.TP
.B \-\-not\-caches
Do not treat cache files as sequences; they are listed
individually as regular files instead. If
.B \-\-only\-sequences
is also given, cache files are omitted from the listing
altogether, since that option suppresses the regular-file output
that
.B \-\-not\-caches
would otherwise fall back to.
.SS Sequence display-modifiers
.TP
.BI \-\-format " FORMAT" ", " \-f " FORMAT"
List sequences in an alternate
.IR FORMAT .
Choices are
.BR native
(default),
.BR nuke ,
.BR rv ,
.BR shake ,
.BR glob ,
.BR mplay ,
and
.BR houdini .
.B glob
only produces correct results when frame numbers are zero-padded.
The error-frame annotations described above are only printed with
.B native
format.
.TP
.BR \-\-prepend\-path\-abs ", " \-p
Prepend the absolute path to each sequence name. Implies
.B \-\-only\-sequences
and suppresses directory-name headers.
.TP
.BR \-\-prepend\-path\-rel ", " \-P
Prepend the relative path to each sequence name. Implies
.B \-\-only\-sequences
and suppresses directory-name headers.
.TP
.BR \-\-extremes ", " \-e
List only the first and last frame of each image or cache sequence,
one per line. Implies
.B \-\-prepend\-path\-abs
(unless
.B \-\-prepend\-path\-rel
is explicitly given), plus
.B \-\-only\-sequences
and
.BR \-\-not\-movies .
.SS Sequence sorting and display
.TP
.BR \-\-recursive ", " \-R
List subdirectories recursively.
.TP
.BR \-\-reverse ", " \-r
Reverse sort order.
.TP
.BR \-\-sort\-by\-time ", " \-t
Sort by timestamp rather than by name. By default, sequences are
compared using the newest frame in each sequence; see
.B \-\-time
to change which frame is used for the comparison, and
.B \-\-which\-time / \-\-ctime / \-\-atime
below to compare using ctime or atime instead of mtime.
.TP
.BI \-\-time " FRAME_AGE"
Which frame within each sequence to use when comparing sequences by
time.
.I FRAME_AGE
is one of
.BR oldest ,
.BR median ,
or
.BR newest
(default).
.TP
.BR \-\-global\-sort\-by\-time ", " \-G
When combined with
.B \-\-prepend\-path\-abs
or
.BR \-\-prepend\-path\-rel ,
sort
.I all
sequences by time across every directory visited, rather than only
within each directory. Ignored otherwise. This is a capability
.BR ls (1)
does not offer.
.TP
.BI \-\-only\-show " TENSE " [CC]YYMMDD[\-hh[mm[ss]]]
List only sequences whose comparison timestamp falls before or
since the given time.
.I TENSE
is
.B before
or
.BR since .
The century
.RI ( CC )
defaults to the current century; hour, minute, and second default to
zero if omitted. Which frame is used for the comparison is governed
by
.BR \-\-time ,
and which underlying timestamp is compared is governed by
.BR \-\-which\-time / \-\-ctime / \-\-atime
below.
.SS Timestamp selection
These options control which underlying file timestamp is used for
every time comparison in
.B lsseq
\(em that is, for
.B \-\-sort\-by\-time
and
.BR \-\-only\-show .
They mirror the
.B \-c
and
.B \-u
flags of
.BR ls (1).
When more than one of these options is given, the last one on the
command line wins, exactly as with
.BR ls (1).
.TP
.BI \-\-which\-time " TIMESTAMP"
Select which timestamp to use.
.I TIMESTAMP
is one of
.BR mtime
(last modification time, default),
.BR ctime
(last change of file status information), or
.BR atime
(last access time).
.TP
.BR \-\-ctime ", " \-c
Use ctime instead of mtime. Equivalent to
.BR "\-\-which\-time ctime" .
.TP
.BR \-\-atime ", " \-u
Use atime instead of mtime. Equivalent to
.BR "\-\-which\-time atime" .
.SS Symbolic-link handling
Regardless of the options below,
.B lsseq
always prints the name of a symbolic link itself, never the file it
points to.
.TP
.BR \-\-dereference\-command\-line ", " \-H
Only follow symbolic links to files and directories named directly
on the command line. (Default.)
.TP
.BR \-\-dereference ", " \-L
Follow all symbolic links, to files and directories alike.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-dereference
Do not follow any symbolic links.
.TP
.B \-\-dereference\-command\-line\-symlink\-to\-dir
Only follow command-line symbolic links that point to directories.
.TP
.B \-\-dereference\-symlink\-to\-dir
Follow all symbolic links that point to directories.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-dereference\-dir
Do not follow symbolic links to directories.
.TP
.B \-\-dereference\-command\-line\-symlink\-to\-file
Only follow command-line symbolic links that point to regular
files.
.TP
.B \-\-dereference\-symlink\-to\-file
Follow all symbolic links that point to regular files.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-dereference\-file
Do not follow symbolic links to regular files.
.SS LS(1) control for non-sequences
.TP
.BR \-\-single ", " \-1
List one non-sequence entry per line (see
.BR ls (1)).
.TP
.BR \-\-all ", " \-a
Do not ignore entries starting with
.RB \(aq . \(aq
(see
.BR ls (1) \|'s
.BR \-\-almost\-all ).
.TP
.BR \-\-by\-columns ", " \-C
List non-sequence entries by columns (see
.BR ls (1)).
.TP
.BR \-\-by\-rows ", " \-x
List non-sequence entries by rows instead of columns (see
.BR ls (1)).
.TP
.BR \-\-directory ", " \-d
List directory entries themselves, not their contents, and do not
follow symbolic links (see
.BR ls (1)).
.TP
.BR \-\-classify ", " \-F
Append an indicator
.RB ( * / = > @ | )
to entries, and do not follow symbolic links to directories (see
.BR ls (1)
for the meaning of each symbol). A sequence made up entirely of
symbolic links is also marked with
.BR @ .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LSSEQ_IMAGE_EXTENSION
Colon-separated list of image file extensions to recognize, in
place of the built-in default list (see
.BR \-\-img\-ext ).
.TP
.B LSSEQ_MOV_EXTENSION
Colon-separated list of movie file extensions to recognize, in
place of the built-in default list.
.TP
.B LSSEQ_CACHE_EXTENSION
Colon-separated list of cache file extensions to recognize, in
place of the built-in default list.
.TP
.BR OIC_IMAGE_EXTENSION ", " OIC_MOV_EXTENSION ", " OIC_CACHE_EXTENSION
Deprecated aliases for the three variables above, kept for backward
compatibility with older configurations and being phased out in
favor of the
.B LSSEQ_*
names.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Exit codes are combined bitwise, so a single run can report more
than one condition at once.
.TP
.B 0
No error.
.TP
.B 1
A call to
.BR ls (1)
reported an error, or another internal issue occurred while
listing.
.TP
.B 2
Bad command-line argument (the standard argparse error code).
.TP
.B 4
Warning: a broken symbolic link was encountered.
.TP
.B 8
Warning: two frames in a sequence share the same frame number but
differ in padding.
.TP
.B 16
Warning: recursive descent into a directory was blocked because the
execute bit was not set.
.TP
.B 32
Warning: a file named explicitly on the command line does not
exist.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
List the current directory, condensing sequences:
.RS
.nf
$ lsseq
.fi
.RE
.TP
Print a sequence in a format suitable for piping to another tool:
.RS
.nf
$ lsseq \-\-format rv
bbb.97\-103@@@.jpg
$ rv `lsseq \-f rv`
.fi
.RE
.TP
List only sequences, with absolute paths, suitable for scripting:
.RS
.nf
$ lsseq \-\-prepend\-path\-abs \-\-format rv *
.fi
.RE
.TP
Sort sequences by time across every directory visited:
.RS
.nf
$ lsseq \-\-global\-sort\-by\-time \-\-recursive \-\-prepend\-path\-abs /projects
.fi
.RE
.TP
Sort by last-access time instead of modification time (added in 5.1.0):
.RS
.nf
$ lsseq \-\-sort\-by\-time \-\-atime
.fi
.RE
.TP
List only sequences whose status changed on or after a given date,
comparing ctime instead of mtime:
.RS
.nf
$ lsseq \-\-ctime \-\-only\-show since 260101
.fi
.RE
.TP
Hide the missing- and zero-length-frame annotations:
.RS
.nf
$ lsseq \-\-skip\-missing \-\-skip\-zero
.fi
.RE
.SH HISTORY
.B lsseq
follows Semantic Versioning 2.0.0.
.PP
As of
.BR 4.0.0 ,
all long options were renamed from camelCase to kebab-case (for
example,
.B \-\-globalSortByTime
became
.BR \-\-global\-sort\-by\-time ),
a breaking change reflected in the major version bump.
.PP
As of
.BR 5.0.0 ,
the
.B \-c
short form of
.B \-\-combine\-lists
was removed, another breaking change, freeing
.B \-c
for reuse.
.PP
As of
.BR 5.1.0 ,
.B \-c
was reintroduced as shorthand for the new
.B \-\-ctime
option, alongside
.B \-u
for
.BR \-\-atime ,
and the new
.B \-\-which\-time
option, adding the ability to sort and filter sequences by ctime or
atime in addition to the default mtime, mirroring
.BR ls (1) \|'s
own
.B \-c
and
.B \-u
flags. This was an additive, backward-compatible change.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ls (1).
.PP
